<p>They&rsquo;ve been sneaking up on me again in my own writing, and I&rsquo;ve been trying to catch them, changing them to colons. That&rsquo;s alright for the most part; it kinda works. Kinda.</p>
<p>And then <a href="https://psychotechnology.substack.com">this guy</a><sup><a id="fnr.1" class="footref" href="#fn.1" role="doc-backlink">1</a></sup><a href="https://psychotechnology.substack.com/p/em-dashes-are-fucking-amazing">came along</a>:</p>
<blockquote>
<p>&hellip;colons are so nearly useless — they are the same width as a comma and unlike periods they don&rsquo;t change the register of the following letter. Em dashes are of a clearly different length. And most colons are better off as em dashes to delineate different parts of the sentence.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>The problem with Em dashes these days is AI of course. Sasha has an answer for this as well:</p>
<blockquote>
<p>I don&rsquo;t give a shit what the &ldquo;oh yeah let&rsquo;s examine this piece of writing with a magnifying glass for any signs of AI&rdquo; crowd thinks. I don&rsquo;t give a shit that AI figured out how to produce em dashes before these online troglodytes did. Typing em dashes is not an AGI-complete problem. It&rsquo;s not that difficult to produce an organic, artisanal, hand-crafted em dash — on Mac, for example, it&rsquo;s simply Option + Shift + Hyphen.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>I&rsquo;ve been using regular dashes as em dashes for years before I even knew they&rsquo;re called em dashes (and then realized that I&rsquo;ve been using regular dashes wrong) and thanks to this guy I learned how to do that on a Mac, and now in my Emacs too: <code>---</code> in org-mode will become em-dashes in a new dwim function I just made<sup><a id="fnr.2" class="footref" href="#fn.2" role="doc-backlink">2</a></sup>.</p>
<p>So yes: someone, at some point, would point to my posts and blame me for spewing AI-made content. <a href="https://taonaw.com/2026/03/22/using-ai-to-edit-and.html">I already confessed I use AI to edit and polish my posts</a>, and also:</p>
<blockquote>
<p>&ldquo;But what would people think of my writing if I ever used an em dash? What if someone points one out in my writing?&rdquo;. Bitch, please. Are you writing for an internet rando who&rsquo;s got nothing better to do than to hunt for em dashes? Fine, you do, okay, fair enough. Then I&rsquo;ve got you covered: use AP-style em dashes, these are separated from the text by spaces instead of being glued to it. That&rsquo;s how every em dash in this piece is styled — and it&rsquo;s unlike AI em dashes which are &ldquo;like—this, word—word&rdquo;.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>So I&rsquo;ve been using AP-style em dashes the whole time? <a href="https://www.apstylebook.com/blog_posts/24">Turns out I did</a>!</p>
<blockquote>
<p>They are about the width of a capital letter M, and always get a space on either side, except in sports agate.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>And while there, I saw they mention AI too (because how can you not):</p>
<blockquote>
<p>It&rsquo;d be nice if there was an easy tell <em>(if something is written by AI or not - JTR)</em>. Unfortunately, that tell is not the em dash, which has been around for many years. In fact, they are so ubiquitous in AP lingo, we just call them dashes.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>OK then, I feel vindicated enough. Sorry Em dashes, it&rsquo;s been too long.</p>
<p>Oh, and while I&rsquo;m on my AI soap box, let me vent about yet another thing that AI is actually messing up for me: commas.</p>
<p>I don&rsquo;t care if I need a comma before &ldquo;too&rdquo; or &ldquo;ok&rdquo; in a sentence like this one here ok?</p>
<p>If I were to write: &ldquo;I don&rsquo;t care if I need a comma before &rsquo;too&rsquo; or &lsquo;ok&rsquo; in a sentence like this one here, ok?&rdquo; with that comma before the ok, it will break the flow. And I like my grumpy flow.</p>
<p>It makes you take a pause, as if the &ldquo;OK&rdquo; is its own thought almost, but it&rsquo;s not. It&rsquo;s part of the whole sentence before it, just as I&rsquo;d say it. I don&rsquo;t <em>care</em> if it&rsquo;s grammatically correct — it&rsquo;s not JTR correct. So screw that. Thanks but not thanks.</p>
